story.append(P('<b>Technical scope:</b> synthetic binary composition and process scenarios; documented NIST pure-component Antoine ranges; hypothetical Margules activity parameter; simplified McCabe-Thiele screening.','p'))
story.append(P('14 November 2026  |  Functionality and scientific feasibility study  |  No performance comparison','small'))
new()
heading('1. Industrial problem and current state of the art')
para('Distillation is a central separation process in chemical manufacturing, solvent recycling, refining and specialty chemicals. A column contacts rising vapor with descending liquid. Reboiler heat, condenser duty, reflux and the internal composition gradient jointly govern whether a target split is feasible. Plant engineers often need to examine many possible recipes and operating conditions without changing the surrounding control or simulation application.')
para('A molecular vapor-liquid equilibrium (VLE) relation expresses the vapor composition corresponding to a liquid composition at specified conditions. Relative volatility is a useful binary shortcut; a more physically connected model evaluates temperature-dependent component vapor pressures and liquid activity coefficients. However, an equilibrium curve on its own cannot replace mass, equilibrium, summation and enthalpy (MESH) equations, tray hydraulic constraints or accurate experimental thermodynamic data.')
para('Modern property packages, including those documented by DWSIM, support ideal-liquid Raoult models and nonideal-liquid activity expressions such as NRTL, Wilson and UNIQUAC. The choice depends on the chemical system and available interaction parameters. Research published in 2025 continues to study the influence of VLE model multiplicity on more rigorous distillation simulations. The present study uses this background to delimit, rather than exaggerate, the contribution of an expression-execution layer.')
sub('The practical research question')
para('Can the original paceval Mathematical Engine execute two independently versioned VLE model families and connected process calculations through reusable computation graphs, while a small C++ host retains only iterative algorithms, input handling and categorical orchestration?')
sub('A concrete hypothetical workflow')
para('An engineer examines a benzene/toluene-inspired binary mixture at different temperatures and feed compositions. A selected VLE model computes vapor enrichment. A material balance defines possible distillate and bottoms flows. A separate, simplified column stepping algorithm estimates idealized equilibrium stages and compares reflux-dependent heat-duty proxies against a declared capacity. Each run is synthetic; no chemical plant or real operating dataset was used.')
sub('Model boundaries')
table([['Included and actually executed','Not represented by this demonstrator'],['Temperature-dependent Antoine relations and explicit liquid activity correction','Measured binary interaction parameters and verified mixture predictions'],['Composition sensitivities and two reusable model versions','Automatic symbolic differentiation'],['Synthetic mass, reflux and heat-duty graph chains','Coupled, energy-balanced industrial MESH column simulation'],['McCabe-Thiele-style equilibrium stage screening','Tray hydraulics, real transfer efficiency and plant certification']],[81*mm,86*mm])
new()
heading('2. Defined thermodynamic models')
para('Version 1 uses the binary relative-volatility law from the original proposal, with a fixed dimensionless parameter alpha = 2.6. Version 2 uses separate pure-component saturation pressures with a hypothetical, positive two-suffix Margules activity correction. The chemistry-related inputs represent benzene (more volatile) and toluene. Their NIST vapor-pressure constants are used only inside the published temperature overlap. The Margules parameter A = 0.18 is illustrative and has not been fitted to experimental binary VLE data.')
story.append(P('v1: y = alpha x / [1 + (alpha - 1) x]','eq'))
story.append(P('log10(Psat,i / bar) = Ai - Bi / (T + Ci), with T in kelvin','eq'))
story.append(P('v2: y = x gammaA Psat,A / [x gammaA Psat,A + (1 - x) gammaB Psat,B]','eq'))
story.append(P('gammaA = exp[A(1-x)^2],    gammaB = exp[A x^2]','eq'))
sub('Published pure-component Antoine constants')
table([['Component','A','B','C','Valid T [K]'],['Benzene','4.72583','1660.652','-1.461','333.4-373.5'],['Toluene','4.07827','1343.943','-53.773','308.52-384.66']],[35*mm,23*mm,30*mm,30*mm,49*mm])
para('For v2 the bubble-pressure expression at composition x is the sum of x gammaA Psat,A and (1-x) gammaB Psat,B. At a fixed total pressure of 0.60 bar, C++ bisection repeatedly evaluates the real paceval bubble-pressure graph until a numerical bubble-temperature root is found. The v1 curve is independent of temperature and remains an approximate composition model. Its physical bubble-temperature context is a common diagnostic and not a closure of the constant-volatility model.')
sub('Analytic sensitivities and domains')
para('Version 1 uses the explicitly differentiated dy/dx = alpha / [1 + (alpha - 1)x]^2. For version 2 the sensitivity is computed from a quotient derivative including the derivative of each activity coefficient, and an additional fixed-composition temperature sensitivity is evaluated from the Antoine pressure derivatives. The temperature-input range is restricted to 333.4-373.5 K and liquid compositions are constrained to [0,1]. Both constraints are enforced before graph evaluation.')
new()
heading('3. Column screening, graph ownership and execution architecture')
para('The column experiment assumes a saturated-liquid feed, constant molar overflow, a total condenser and equilibrium stages. These assumptions permit rectifying and stripping operating-line equations and the approximate McCabe-Thiele graphical stepping procedure. They do not establish a complete physical plant energy balance.')
story.append(P('F = D + B     and     F zF = D xD + B xB','eq'))
story.append(P('L = R D,       V = (R + 1) D,       Qreb = V Hvap / 3.6','eq'))
para('Here F, D, B, L and V are molar flows in kmol/h. The selected constant latent-heat proxy is 31 MJ/kmol; therefore Qreb is in kW. A corresponding condenser proxy has equal magnitude and opposite sign. The symbol is an energy-consumption proxy, not a full enthalpy residual.')
sub('Actual mathematical execution')
table([['Processing stage','Execution owner','Verification'],['VLE, vapor enrichment and temperature pressure sensitivities','Persistent paceval graph handles','Separate native C++ plus 80-digit reference'],['Bubble pressure and isobaric root function','paceval graph, host bisection','Separate Python root finder'],['Product split and component balances','paceval D and B graphs','Analytic material balance'],['Liquid reflux and vapor flow','paceval L and V graphs, using previous graph results','Independent mass and process flow'],['Heat-duty, purity, rectifying/stripping lines, numeric guards','paceval composite graphs','Separate independent reference'],['Stage-count decisions and CSV provenance','C++ host consuming paceval VLE values','Independent algorithm and schema checks']],[56*mm,55*mm,56*mm])
para('The integration uses the official paceval C++ API, not a simulated mathematical library. It exercises persistent graph creation, scalar evaluation, multi-graph batch evaluation and deletion of handles. Both model families remain independently addressable. The C++ host never substitutes a precomputed native result for the production mathematical graph outputs.')
new()
heading('4. Executed workload and independent verification')
para(f'The archived experiment performed {E["input_pairs"]:,} synthetic temperature/composition input evaluations in each of two versions, for {E["model_point_cases"]:,} model-point cases. It also evaluated {E["stagewise_configurations"]:,} separate synthetic column configurations. The original paceval library executed {E["unique_graphs_executed"]} distinct registered graphs, {E["multi_graph_batch_calls"]:,} multi-graph batch calls and {E["graph_point_evaluations"]:,} graph-point outputs. Of the 73 executed graphs, 63 are domain functions in the published formula registry; 10 are operator and API-layout self-test graphs. Scalar calls and algorithmic root evaluations are counted separately in the machine-readable execution evidence.')
table([['Reproducible experiment field','Archived outcome'],['Distinct graphs created / executed',f'{E["unique_graphs_created"]} / {E["unique_graphs_executed"]}'],['Versioned model-point cases',f'{E["model_point_cases"]:,}'],['Column screening configurations',f'{E["stagewise_configurations"]:,}'],['Combined scalar paceval calls',f'{E["scalar_calls"]:,}'],['Multi-graph API calls',f'{E["multi_graph_batch_calls"]:,}'],['Executed graph-point outputs',f'{E["graph_point_evaluations"]:,}'],['Independent native mathematical checks',f'{E["native_reference_checks"]:,}'],['Separate 80-decimal-digit check values',str(Q['independent_high_precision_values_checked'])]],[91*mm,76*mm])
para('Independent validation is not an endorsement of the assumed mixture properties. In addition to full-run C++ mathematical checks, a separately programmed Python reference uses 80 decimal digits for selected equilibrium and derivative results. A different numerical root solver checks bubble temperatures, and all archived column records are checked for total and component mass conservation and the stated heat-duty definition. Detailed numeric differences remain in the included technical audit files rather than the public scientific figures.')
para('The CSV path uses the original paceval floating-point string conversion functions. It rejects expressions accidentally placed into numeric fields, nonfinite values, invalid literal syntax and temperature/composition inputs outside the studied domain. The exact archive includes the registered mathematical formulas and their provenance.')
new()
heading('5. Scientific results and interpretation')
para('The model versions differ because the second model incorporates temperature and composition effects rather than using a fixed alpha. Their outputs should not be interpreted as validation of two competing industrial property models: version 2 intentionally has a hypothetical binary interaction parameter and both models operate on synthetic inputs.')
table([['Measured from executed archive','Version v1','Version v2'],['Mean vapor benzene fraction',f'{float(V[0]["mean_vapor_y"]):.6f}',f'{float(V[1]["mean_vapor_y"]):.6f}'],['Mean vapor enrichment (y - x)',f'{float(V[0]["mean_enrichment"]):.6f}',f'{float(V[1]["mean_enrichment"]):.6f}'],['Mean effective relative volatility',f'{float(V[0]["mean_alpha_eff"]):.6f}',f'{float(V[1]["mean_alpha_eff"]):.6f}'],['Mean composition sensitivity',f'{float(V[0]["mean_dy_dx"]):.6f}',f'{float(V[1]["mean_dy_dx"]):.6f}'],['Synthetic guard-positive records',V[0]['indicator_count'],V[1]['indicator_count']]],[69*mm,49*mm,49*mm])
para('The same material-balanced synthetic mass-flow policy and the same prescribed 31 MJ/kmol latent-heat proxy were used in both versions. Consequently, an equal mean heat-duty proxy across versions is expected and is not a claim that VLE model choice never affects real distillation energy use. A rigorous design would solve operating temperatures, phase compositions and stage enthalpies together.')
para('For the stagewise illustration, model v1 had a mean of 7.43 calculated idealized stages across the chosen configurations, whereas model v2 had 7.11. These numbers are outcomes of the simplified stepping algorithm and the declared feed, composition and reflux choices only. They are not numbers of real trays or a measured industrial capacity improvement.')
heading('6. Scientific figures from actual paceval outputs')
para('All numbered charts on the following pages were generated from the archived paceval-executed CSV result files. The cover illustration is the only non-numerical artwork. The equations in the source C++ are available in machine-readable form in the formula registry.')
